ion when pulling poly extraction results, or uneven load distribution across the threads.\n\nThe poly extraction optimization did move a lot of work to the GPU, but there's still a single batch D2H copy at the end, and I'm wondering if the biggest single contributor to this overhead is something I haven't fully isolated yet.\n\nLooking at where the real time is going, LogUp GKR dominates at 529 milliseconds—even a 20% improvement there would save 106 milliseconds, which is more than enough to reach the 2x speedup target. The fractional sumcheck inside the GKR loop is the main culprit at 212 milliseconds of GPU time, running sequentially through about 70 rounds per segment across 7 levels. Most rounds are just the compute_round_and_revert kernel at 0.5 milliseconds each, but the level transitions add multifold and precompute operations that together take 6.6 milliseconds per level.\n\nMy rough math suggests 140 rounds at 0.5 milliseconds plus 14 level transitions at 6.6 milliseconds should total around 162 milliseconds, but the measured time is 212 milliseconds—there's a 50-millisecond gap I need to account for, either from overhead or inaccurate estimates. The input evaluation component sits around 300 milliseconds, so that's another significant piece. Now I'm digging into Trace Commit, which shows 253 milliseconds at APC 300, with the poseidon2 hashing kernel taking up most of that at 231 milliseconds across 25 instances, though I need to verify whether all the calculate_zero_hash time is actually part of Trace Commit or if some of it belongs elsewhere.\n\nLooking at the calculate_zero_hash calls—there are 584 instances running at about 186 microseconds each, totaling 109 milliseconds, and these might be parallelizable or batchable. I'm trying to understand whether each AIR computes its own hash or if the traces are stacked together, so I should check the actual code to clarify the architecture. Rather than getting lost in the details, I should focus on what optimizations are actually feasible—I've already tried multi-streaming for GPU parallelism, batched kernel launches to reduce per-AIR overhead, and pre-allocation, so I need to figure out what other approaches could close that remaining 74-millisecond gap.\n\nThe poseidon2 hashing at 231 milliseconds and the zero hash computation at 109 milliseconds are both part of Trace Commit and haven't been optimized yet, which could be a significant opportunity.\n\nFor the GKR fractional sumcheck, there's 212 milliseconds of sequential GPU work per round where I might reduce overhead between kernels, though previous attempts to move transcript processing to the GPU didn't help because the CPU work was already fast.\n\nKernel fusion could reduce launch overhead by combining multiple small kernels within a round, and the WHIR optimization at 99-218 milliseconds scales well enough that it's not the bottleneck. The parallel Poseidon2 hashing across 25 kernel instances in Trace Commit is another area worth exploring.\n\nLooking more carefully at the profiling data, I need to distinguish which kernels are actually part of STARK excluding trace generation — the Poseidon2 row hashing at 231 milliseconds is the Merkle tree leaf hashing for trace commitment, and the zero hash computation at 109 milliseconds across 584 instances is likely the hash tree computation, both of which could potentially be batched or cached to reduce overhead. with AIR count. Let me look at the actual code to understand where `calculate_zero_hash` fits in the pipeline.\n\nI have enough context now to start brainstorming solutions. The goal is clear: shave about 74ms off the 1301ms STARK exclusion time at APC 300 to hit the 1227ms target.\n\nOne promising approach is overlapping Trace Commit with the early stages of Constraints work — instead of waiting for all trace segments to commit before starting constraint rounds, I could begin Round 0 on early segments while still committing the later ones. This would hide some