ilarly: more AIRs means more small traces, and the batch overhead plus small-trace inefficiency increases with each round. Stacked Reduction has the same problem — it processes all stacked traces with fixed per-trace overhead regardless of size.\n\nThe common thread is that per-AIR or per-trace fixed overhead doesn't shrink as individual traces get smaller, so I need to focus on reducing that overhead.\n\nLooking at where the biggest absolute savings are, MLE Rounds is the culprit — it takes 159ms at APC 300 but only 113ms at APC 0, which is backwards given the 2.3x fewer cells. The excess overhead is around 91-108ms depending on how I calculate perfect scaling. With 623 AIRs versus 99, each MLE round processes far more traces, creating more batch descriptors and kernel launches. Smaller traces mean less GPU work per trace and worse utilization. I'm checking the kernel timing data from nsight to understand where exactly the time is going. Looking at the per-round overhead in MLE, the CPU work for polynomial batching and transcript operations across ~20 rounds probably contributes around 20ms total, while kernel launch overhead is negligible at under 2ms. The real bottleneck seems to be that with 623 AIRs, each batch kernel processes larger batches, creating more block launches and per-kernel overhead.\n\nNow examining the timing breakdown: at APC 300, the 159ms across ~20 rounds works out to roughly 8ms per round with 5-6 kernel launches each, while at APC 0 with 113ms over ~16 rounds gives about 7ms per round. The per-round time is comparable, but I need to reconsider what determines n_max—it's based on the tallest trace height after sorting, so both configurations should have similar maximum iteration counts despite their different AIR counts.\n\nLooking at the actual measurements more carefully, if n_max is around 17 for both, then the per-round cost at APC 300 is roughly 9.4ms versus 6.6ms at APC 0, suggesting the extra 48ms overhead comes from processing 623 AIRs instead of 99. The batched MLE kernels use descriptor arrays that scale with AIR count, so there's likely overhead in managing those larger descriptor sets across all the rounds.\n\nWhat if we could fuse multiple MLE round iterations together? Instead of launching separate GPU kernels for each round with CPU synchronization between them, we could run several rounds' worth of GPU work back-to-back. The challenge is that each round depends on the previous round's Fiat-Shamir challenge, but the sumcheck protocol structure might allow us to pipeline this—the prover sends evaluations, the verifier computes the challenge, and the prover folds for the next round, all potentially without CPU round-trips in between.\n\nLooking at the actual code flow, each round requires CPU work to compute the batch polynomial from per-trace evaluations, observe them in the transcript, and sample the next challenge before folding on the GPU. The key insight is that the fold operation could potentially overlap with the next round's preparation if we can decouple the CPU transcript logic from the GPU kernel launches. Looking at the actual execution flow, the GPU evaluation finishes and syncs back to CPU before any transcript work happens, which means the GPU sits idle while I'm computing the batch polynomial and sampling the challenge. The fold can't start until I have that challenge value, so there's no way to overlap these operations—the dependency chain is strict: evaluate, sync, compute challenge, then fold.\n\nBut I could potentially overlap the fold computation with preparing descriptors for the next round, since the fold kernels might still be running asynchronously on the GPU while the CPU moves ahead. Though actually, the next round's evaluation needs the folded matrices as input, so that won't work either.\n\nLet me explore a different approach entirely—what if I precompute something about the trace commit to reduce overhead?\n\nThe stacked matrix gets padded with zeros for short