 The untouched components are WHIR at 99ms and the trace commit RS encoding, but WHIR already scales well at 2.20x. The real constraint is getting STARK excluding trace down to a specific target at APC 300, and the stacked reduction CPU NTT is the clearest remaining bottleneck to attack.\n\nNow examining the kernel profiling data more closely, I'm noticing something interesting in the GKR inner loop—the compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel is running 1370 times but each instance is extremely fast at 6 microseconds, while compute_round_and_revert_kernel is much slower at 70ms across 141 instances.\n\nLooking at the full breakdown of GPU time across all GKR kernels, the major contributors are compute_round_and_revert_kernel at 70ms, bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel at 50ms, multifold_kernel at 47ms, and precompute_m_build_partial_kernel at 45ms, with several other kernels contributing smaller amounts.\n\nThe total kernel time adds up to roughly 224ms, which combined with D2H transfer and CPU overhead brings the main GKR loop to about 260ms, and when including the input evaluation phase, the whole thing reaches around 529ms. Since these kernels run sequentially, the wall time is essentially the sum of all kernel times.\n\nThe biggest opportunity for optimization is the 50ms bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel that handles initial tree building—I'm wondering if this could be overlapped with the GKR input evaluation phase to hide some of that latency.\n\nLooking at the actual execution flow, the GKR input evaluation completes first and returns the full inputs buffer, then fractional_sumcheck_gpu immediately starts building the segment tree on that buffer. Since the tree building depends on having all inputs ready, I can't start it early. But the input evaluation itself runs across 8 threads, so theoretically if one thread finishes before others, I could begin tree construction on the partial data—though that would require restructuring how the inputs are produced and consumed.\n\nActually, pipelining the segment tree building on partial inputs seems too fragile and complex. Let me refocus on the Stacked Reduction instead, which is already at 74ms after several optimizations: eliminating per-window D2H syncs brought it from 311ms to 126ms, then batching the MLE round kernels got it down to 75ms. The multi-stream approach failed due to buffer allocation issues, so I need to look at what's still bottlenecking the remaining time.\n\nThe 74ms breaks down into roughly 38ms of GPU kernel time and 36ms of CPU NTT for polynomial reconstruction. That CPU NTT is nearly half the total, so moving it to the GPU would be the single biggest win for Stacked Reduction. But even that alone won't get me to the 74ms target unless there are other hidden savings I'm missing.\n\nActually, what if I could overlap Stacked Reduction with the later MLE rounds instead of running them sequentially? The constraint is that Stacked Reduction needs the folded evaluation matrices from MLE, but within those rounds, traces reach full folding at different points—maybe I could start reconstructing the fully-folded traces early while MLE is still processing the others.\n\nLet me shift focus to the GKR input eval phase, which is taking about 269ms. The GPU kernel time totals 538ms across 8 streams, which theoretically should be only 67ms wall time, but we're seeing 4x worse performance. This gap likely comes from load imbalance across threads, per-AIR overhead like buffer setup and kernel launches, memory bandwidth contention between the 8 concurrent streams, or CUDA driver overhead. The pre-allocation work brought it down from much higher to 318ms, and the round-robin approach got it to 296ms, so there's still room to optimize.\n\nThe 203ms gap between theoretical minimum and actual time is substantial. Kernel launch overhead is negligible at ~0.3ms total. Buffer setup with pre-allocation should just be pointer assignments. The real culprits are likely CUDA driver serialization when multiple threads submit to different streams, and memory bandwidth saturation since all 8 threads are reading from different trace matrices simultaneously—with 1TB/s available bandwidth, this could be the bottleneck.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers: 472ms GPU time across 486 instances means 0.97ms per instance on average, but there's huge variance from 67μs to 16ms. The largest instances dominate the timeline. With height-descending sort distributing large AIRs round-robin across 8 threads, a thread might get a few 16ms instances plus ~60 smaller ones averaging 0.3ms each, totaling around 34ms per thread. But GPU execution isn't just submit-and-forget—multiple kernels occupying SMs simultaneously across 8 streams creates contention that extends the wall time beyond that estimate.\n\nThe RTX 4090 has 128 SMs, and a large kernel using all of them blocks other streams' kernels from starting. So multi-streaming only helps when small kernels can fill gaps between large ones; if large kernels saturate the SMs, the benefit diminishes. At APC 300, there are a few massive AIRs like Poseidon2Air with height 2^16 alongside many smaller ones, which means SM contention is likely the bottleneck rather than thread load imbalance.\n\nAfter weighing the options, I'm going to focus on moving the CPU-side polynomial reconstruction in Stacked Reduction to the GPU—that ~36ms CPU NTT bottleneck seems like the biggest win. Let me verify the actual cost and what the CPU is doing there.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10468,"cache_read_input_tokens":96354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10468},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00f14df6-1cef-48ea-af88-42c3b73a9be4","uuid":"5b0cec42-9d9a-4562-93b8-d3447b3951c6"}
