the STARK exclusivity trace metric is inflated because it includes GPU work from async kernel launches, making it unreliable. Adding a synchronization point would fix the metric accuracy, but it would also serialize the trace generation's CPU work and GPU work, eliminating any parallelism between them and increasing total proof time. The sync would need to happen after all GPU kernels are launched, so it's really just waiting for the GPU to finish what's already queued.\n\nActually, I realize the sync doesn't change the total proof time at all—the GPU work still happens, it's just measured differently. The wait time that was previously hidden in the Trace Commit span would now be visible. So I can add the sync for measurement accuracy without hurting performance, and then separately work on reducing the actual GPU work through constraints or openings optimization.\n\nLooking at the performance breakdown across APC levels, I'm comparing the STARK exclusive trace times without pipeline stall. At APC 300 the total is around 1140ms, while at APC 0 it's roughly 2136ms—about 1.87x slower. To hit my target of 2x difference, I need to compress the work down further, which means I should focus on whichever component scales most with APC. Looking at the GPU trace generation kernels more carefully—the apc_apply_bus_kernel takes 609ms across 583 instances, while other trace gen kernels add another 50-100ms. The CPU-side trace generation for both segments totals 1239ms, and since the GPU can work concurrently, it might finish its ~700ms of work before the CPU finishes, meaning no pipeline stall when STARK begins. But I need to clarify which kernels are which—the apc_tracegen_kernel at 18ms seems separate from the apc_apply_bus_kernel, so I'm getting confused about the kernel breakdown.\n\nActually, the apc_apply_bus_kernel is part of trace generation itself, applying autoprecompile bus rules, so that 609ms is significant GPU time. The real question is when these kernels complete