ect access to the device to call sync on it.\n\nWait, actually looking at the Coordinator struct, `self.device` is a field that's accessible, so I could call `self.device.sync()` directly if the device type implements a sync method. The device is generic parameter PD which is bounded by `ProverDevice<PB, TS>`, so I'd need to add a sync method requirement to that trait.\n\nThe cleanest approach would be adding a `pre_prove_sync()` method to the `ProverDevice` trait with a default no-op implementation, then override it in the GPU device to actually sync. This keeps CPU behavior unchanged while ensuring GPU operations are drained before timing starts, making the STARK exclusive trace metric accurate.\n\nBut I'm realizing this is really a measurement fix rather than an optimization—it doesn't make the proof faster, just redistributes time between metrics. To properly address the scaling issue, I should combine the sync fix with an actual optimization that reduces GPU work somewhere, like in GKR or another component.\n\nLooking at the per-segment timing data, I can see the actual STARK work is around 1112ms at APC 300 versus 2040ms at APC 0, giving me a ratio of about 1.83x. That tells me I need to find roughly another 1112ms of work reduction to hit the target.\n\nIf I can shave 92ms off the APC 300 time, I'd reach 1020ms and get that clean 2.0x ratio. The LogUp GKR component at 529ms could yield about 90ms with a 17% improvement by reducing per-AIR overhead in the GPU kernel evaluation—the current wall time is around 270ms when the kernel itself only takes 67ms with perfect load balancing, so there's clearly overhead I can address.\n\nScaling from 8 to 16 threads could help distribute the 623 AIRs more evenly (dropping from 78 per thread to 39), which should improve load balance and GPU stream scheduling. The tradeoff is more mutex contention and memory overhead, but I'm estimating this could save another 27-40ms on the GKR input eval.\n\nAlternatively, I could batch s