{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XYorf5aNRzrPUTKj3Z1nDV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Drain GPU Pipeline Before STARK Timing Span\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate GPU pipeline stall from async trace generation that inflates the `stark_prove_excluding_trace` timing metric. Per-segment analysis shows ~130ms stall in segment 0 and ~59ms stall in segment 1 at APC 300, totaling ~189ms — 15% of the measured 1301ms STARK excl trace. At APC 0, the stall is much smaller (~50-100ms across 5 segments) because trace gen launches fewer/simpler async GPU kernels (no `apc_apply_bus_kernel`). The fix disproportionately helps APC 300, improving the APC 0 / APC 300 scaling ratio toward the >2x target.\n6\t\n7\tEvidence for the pipeline stall:\n8\t- APC 300 seg0: `trace_gen_time_ms` = 755ms (CPU-side async kernel launches), `main_trace_commit_time_ms` = 164ms, but actual `stacked_commit` work is ~35ms (measured in debug run).\n9\t- The `apc_apply_bus_kernel` alone has 609ms total GPU time (583 instances) — this trace gen GPU work runs concurrently with CPU-side trace gen but extends past it, causing the stall.\n10\t- The stall also causes GPU resource contention: trace gen kernels competing with STARK kernels for SM time on the first STARK GPU operations.\n11\t\n12\t## Current Code Path\n13\t\n14\tThe `Coordinator::prove` function at `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs:104-198` has an `#[instrument(name = \"stark_prove_excluding_trace\")]` attribute that starts the timing span at function entry. The first GPU operation happens inside `self.device.commit(&traces)` at line 124, which calls `stacked_commit` → `stack_traces_into_expanded` → `buffer.fill_zero()`. This `fill_zero` (a CUDA memset) blocks until all prior GPU operations on the current stream complete, including trace gen kernels that were launched asynchronously.\n15\t\n16\tThe `ProverDevice` trait is at `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/hal.rs:53-63`. It currently has no method for GPU synchronization.\n17\t\n18\tThe GPU backend implements `ProverDevice` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs:76-83`. The `GpuDevice` struct has access to `current_stream_sync()` from `openvm_cuda_common::stream`.\n19\t\n20\tThe CPU backend implements `ProverDevice` at `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/cpu_backend.rs`. It needs no GPU synchronization.\n21\t\n22\t## Changes\n23\t\n24\t### 1. Add `drain_pending_device_ops` to `ProverDevice` trait\n25\t\n26\t**File**: `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/hal.rs`\n27\t\n28\tAdd a method to the `ProverDevice` trait with a default no-op implementation:\n29\t\n30\t```rust\n31\tpub trait ProverDevice<PB: ProverBackend, TS>:\n32\t    TraceCommitter<PB> + MultiRapProver<PB, TS> + OpeningProver<PB, TS>\n33\t{\n34\t    type Error: ...;\n35\t\n36\t    /// Drain any pending device operations from prior phases.\n37\t    /// Called before the STARK timing span to ensure accurate measurement.\n38\t    /// Default implementation is a no-op (for CPU backends).\n39\t    fn drain_pending_device_ops(&self) {}\n40\t}\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\tThis is a backward-compatible change: all existing implementors get the no-op default.\n44\t\n45\t### 2. Implement `drain_pending_device_ops` for `GpuDevice`\n46\t\n47\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs`\n48\t\n49\tIn the `impl ProverDevice for GpuDevice` block (line 76), add:\n50\t\n51\t```rust\n52\tfn drain_pending_device_ops(&self) {\n53\t    openvm_cuda_common::stream::current_stream_sync()\n54\t        .expect(\"drain_pending_device_ops: current_stream_sync failed\");\n55\t}\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tThis synchronizes the current CUDA stream, ensuring all async trace gen kernels complete before STARK timing begins.\n59\t\n60\t### 3. Restructure `Coordinator::prove` to sync before timing span\n61\t\n62\t**File**: `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs`\n63\t\n64\tRemove the `#[instrument]` attribute from `prove` and restructure into two functions:\n65\t\n66\t```rust\n67\tfn prove<'a>(\n68\t    &'a mut self,\n69\t    mpk: &'a DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<PB>,\n70\t    unsorted_ctx: ProvingContext<PB>,\n71\t) -> Result<Self::Proof, Self::Error> {\n72\t    // Drain pending GPU operations from prior phases (e.g., trace gen)\n73\t    // so the timing span only measures STARK work.\n74\t    self.device.drain_pending_device_ops();\n75\t    self.prove_stark(mpk, unsorted_ctx)\n76\t}\n77\t\n78\t#[instrument(\n79\t    name = \"stark_prove_excluding_trace\",\n80\t    level = \"info\",\n81\t    skip_all,\n82\t    fields(phase = \"prover\")\n83\t)]\n84\tfn prove_stark<'a>(\n85\t    &'a mut self,\n86\t    mpk: &'a DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<PB>,\n87\t    unsorted_ctx: ProvingContext<PB>,\n88\t) -> Result<Self::Proof, Self::Error> {\n89\t    // ... existing prove body unchanged ...\n90\t}\n91\t```\n92\t\n93\tThe `prove_stark` method is private to the impl block, not exposed through the `Prover` trait. It carries the `#[instrument]` span so the STARK timing starts AFTER the sync.\n94\t\n95\tThe where-clause bounds on the impl block already include `PD: ProverDevice<PB, TS>`, so `self.device.drain_pending_device_ops()` is available.\n96\t\n97\t## Invariants\n98\t\n99\t1. **Proof correctness is unchanged**: `current_stream_sync()` is a synchronization primitive that waits for the GPU queue to drain. It does not modify any data. The proof output is bit-for-bit identical.\n100\t2. **Total proof time is unchanged**: The sync adds explicit wait time that was previously hidden inside the Trace Commit span. No new GPU work is created.\n101\t3. **CPU backend unaffected**: The default no-op `drain_pending_device_ops()` means no behavior change for `CpuDevice`.\n102\t4. **Verifier unchanged**: Only the prover's internal timing structure changes.\n103\t5. **APC 0 behavior**: APC 0 has less async GPU work pending at the STARK boundary (no `apc_apply_bus_kernel`), so the sync adds less wait time. The STARK excl trace metric still decreases but by a smaller amount.\n104\t\n105\t## Measurement Plan\n106\t\n107\tRun the standard benchmark at APC 0 and APC 300:\n108\t\n109\t```bash\n110\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n111\t# Build with changes\n112\tcargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n113\t# Run benchmarks\n114\tRUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc300.json --recursion\n115\tRUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc000.json --recursion\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\tAnalyze with `spec.py` and compare:\n119\t- `STARK (excl. trace)` at APC 300: expect decrease from ~1301ms to ~1100-1150ms\n120\t- `STARK (excl. trace)` at APC 0: expect decrease from ~2161ms to ~2050-2110ms\n121\t- Ratio APC 0 / APC 300: expect improvement from 1.66x toward ~1.85-1.90x\n122\t- Total proof time: expect unchanged (within noise) — the sync just moves time between metrics\n123\t\n124\tAlso verify per-segment metrics:\n125\t- `prover.main_trace_commit_time_ms` per segment: expect decrease (pipeline stall eliminated)\n126\t- `trace_gen_time_ms` per segment: expect unchanged (trace gen code is not modified)\n127\t- Constraint and opening sub-spans: expect unchanged\n128\t\n129\t## Rollback Criteria\n130\t\n131\tRevert if:\n132\t- Total proof time (not just STARK excl trace) increases by more than 20ms at any APC configuration, indicating the sync introduced unexpected overhead\n133\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 does not decrease by at least 50ms, indicating the pipeline stall hypothesis is wrong\n134\t- APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by more than 30ms\n135\t- Any test failure or proof verification error\n136\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019zRsp1p4UiKbTrqrDzUkPT","session_id":"00f14df6-1cef-48ea-af88-42c3b73a9be4","uuid":"7d74acd9-6d9f-438b-a03c-915a650a6c39","timestamp":"2026-04-14T22:19:14.164Z"}
