{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FoCW465Jp3JAFHoBqfSwpS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019noNhdEiv7oXe59uwGMH6F","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-0030-drain-gpu-pipeline-before-stark/plan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1589,"cache_read_input_tokens":9284,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1589,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BWeqBXsrkLnfa6kJtKJkxD","session_id":"00f14df6-1cef-48ea-af88-42c3b73a9be4","uuid":"2c81cfc4-a8fd-45ed-97d1-5537f069503b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019noNhdEiv7oXe59uwGMH6F","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Drain GPU Pipeline Before STARK Timing Span\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate GPU pipeline stall from async trace generation that inflates the `stark_prove_excluding_trace` timing metric. Per-segment analysis shows ~130ms stall in segment 0 and ~59ms stall in segment 1 at APC 300, totaling ~189ms — 15% of the measured 1301ms STARK excl trace. At APC 0, the stall is much smaller (~50-100ms across 5 segments) because trace gen launches fewer/simpler async GPU kernels (no `apc_apply_bus_kernel`). The fix disproportionately helps APC 300, improving the APC 0 / APC 300 scaling ratio toward the >2x target.\n6\t\n7\tEvidence for the pipeline stall:\n8\t- APC 300 seg0: `trace_gen_time_ms` = 755ms (CPU-side async kernel launches), `main_trace_commit_time_ms` = 164ms, but actual `stacked_commit` work is ~35ms (measured in debug run).\n9\t- The `apc_apply_bus_kernel` alone has 609ms total GPU time (583 instances) — this trace gen GPU work runs concurrently with CPU-side trace gen but extends past it, causing the stall.\n10\t- The stall also causes GPU resource contention: trace gen kernels competing with STARK kernels for SM time on the first STARK GPU operations.\n11\t\n12\t## Current Code Path\n13\t\n14\tThe `Coordinator::prove` function at `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs:104-198` has an `#[instrument(name = \"stark_prove_excluding_trace\")]` attribute that starts the timing span at function entry. Inside the `stacked_commit` call chain, stream-ordered GPU operations (memsets, kernel launches) are enqueued after any pending trace gen kernels on the same per-thread CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`). The CPU-side synchronization that actually blocks occurs at `MerkleTreeGpu::root()` → `to_host()` (at `crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs:163`), which performs `cudaEventSynchronize` — this is where the CPU waits for ALL prior stream work to complete, including pending trace gen kernels.\n15\t\n16\t**Single-stream assumption**: This plan depends on trace gen kernels being launched on the same `cudaStreamPerThread` stream as the STARK prover (i.e., on the same OS thread). The measured stall inside `stacked_commit` (164ms span time vs ~35ms actual work in isolated runs) is empirical evidence that this is the case — if trace gen used different streams, `to_host()` would not block on them.\n17\t\n18\tThe `ProverDevice` trait is at `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/hal.rs:53-63`. It currently has no method for GPU synchronization.\n19\t\n20\tThe GPU backend implements `ProverDevice` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs:76-83`. The `GpuDevice` struct has access to `current_stream_sync()` from `openvm_cuda_common::stream`.\n21\t\n22\tThe CPU backend implements `ProverDevice` at `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/cpu_backend.rs`. It needs no GPU synchronization.\n23\t\n24\t## Changes\n25\t\n26\t### 1. Add `drain_pending_device_ops` to `ProverDevice` trait\n27\t\n28\t**File**: `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/hal.rs`\n29\t\n30\tAdd a method to the `ProverDevice` trait with a default no-op implementation:\n31\t\n32\t```rust\n33\tpub trait ProverDevice<PB: ProverBackend, TS>:\n34\t    TraceCommitter<PB> + MultiRapProver<PB, TS> + OpeningProver<PB, TS>\n35\t{\n36\t    type Error: ...;\n37\t\n38\t    /// Drain any pending device operations from prior phases.\n39\t    /// Called before the STARK timing span to ensure accurate measurement.\n40\t    /// Default implementation is a no-op (for CPU backends).\n41\t    fn drain_pending_device_ops(&self) {}\n42\t}\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\tThis is a backward-compatible change: all existing implementors get the no-op default.\n46\t\n47\t### 2. Implement `drain_pending_device_ops` for `GpuDevice`\n48\t\n49\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/gpu_backend.rs`\n50\t\n51\tIn the `impl ProverDevice for GpuDevice` block (line 76), add:\n52\t\n53\t```rust\n54\tfn drain_pending_device_ops(&self) {\n55\t    openvm_cuda_common::stream::current_stream_sync()\n56\t        .expect(\"drain_pending_device_ops: current_stream_sync failed\");\n57\t}\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\tThis synchronizes the current CUDA stream, ensuring all async trace gen kernels complete before STARK timing begins.\n61\t\n62\t### 3. Restructure `Coordinator::prove` to sync before timing span\n63\t\n64\t**File**: `crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs`\n65\t\n66\tRemove the `#[instrument]` attribute from `prove` and restructure into two functions:\n67\t\n68\t```rust\n69\tfn prove<'a>(\n70\t    &'a mut self,\n71\t    mpk: &'a DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<PB>,\n72\t    unsorted_ctx: ProvingContext<PB>,\n73\t) -> Result<Self::Proof, Self::Error> {\n74\t    // Drain pending GPU operations from prior phases (e.g., trace gen)\n75\t    // so the timing span only measures STARK work.\n76\t    let _drain_span = info_span!(\"prover.drain_pipeline\", phase = \"prover\").entered();\n77\t    self.device.drain_pending_device_ops();\n78\t    drop(_drain_span);\n79\t    self.prove_stark(mpk, unsorted_ctx)\n80\t}\n81\t\n82\t#[instrument(\n83\t    name = \"stark_prove_excluding_trace\",\n84\t    level = \"info\",\n85\t    skip_all,\n86\t    fields(phase = \"prover\")\n87\t)]\n88\tfn prove_stark<'a>(\n89\t    &'a mut self,\n90\t    mpk: &'a DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<PB>,\n91\t    unsorted_ctx: ProvingContext<PB>,\n92\t) -> Result<Self::Proof, Self::Error> {\n93\t    // ... existing prove body unchanged ...\n94\t}\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\tThe `prove_stark` method is private to the impl block, not exposed through the `Prover` trait. It carries the `#[instrument]` span so the STARK timing starts AFTER the sync.\n98\t\n99\tThe where-clause bounds on the impl block already include `PD: ProverDevice<PB, TS>`, so `self.device.drain_pending_device_ops()` is available.\n100\t\n101\t## Invariants\n102\t\n103\t1. **Proof correctness is unchanged**: `current_stream_sync()` is a synchronization primitive that waits for the GPU queue to drain. It does not modify any data. The proof output is bit-for-bit identical.\n104\t2. **Total proof time is unchanged**: The sync adds explicit wait time that was previously hidden inside the Trace Commit span. No new GPU work is created.\n105\t3. **CPU backend unaffected**: The default no-op `drain_pending_device_ops()` means no behavior change for `CpuDevice`.\n106\t4. **Verifier unchanged**: Only the prover's internal timing structure changes.\n107\t5. **APC 0 behavior**: APC 0 has less async GPU work pending at the STARK boundary (no `apc_apply_bus_kernel`), so the sync adds less wait time. The STARK excl trace metric still decreases but by a smaller amount.\n108\t\n109\t## Measurement Plan\n110\t\n111\tRun the standard benchmark at APC 0 and APC 300:\n112\t\n113\t```bash\n114\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n115\t# Build with changes\n116\tcargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n117\t# Run benchmarks\n118\tRUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc300.json --recursion\n119\tRUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics after_apc000.json --recursion\n120\t```\n121\t\n122\tAnalyze with `spec.py` and compare:\n123\t- `STARK (excl. trace)` at APC 300: expect decrease from ~1301ms to ~1100-1150ms\n124\t- `STARK (excl. trace)` at APC 0: expect decrease from ~2161ms to ~2050-2110ms\n125\t- Ratio APC 0 / APC 300: expect improvement from 1.66x toward ~1.85-1.90x\n126\t- Total proof time: expect unchanged (within noise) — the sync just moves time between metrics\n127\t\n128\tAlso verify per-segment metrics:\n129\t- `prover.main_trace_commit_time_ms` per segment: expect decrease (pipeline stall eliminated)\n130\t- `trace_gen_time_ms` per segment: expect unchanged (trace gen code is not modified)\n131\t- Constraint and opening sub-spans: expect unchanged\n132\t\n133\t## Rollback Criteria\n134\t\n135\tRevert if:\n136\t- Total proof time (not just STARK excl trace) increases by more than 20ms at any APC configuration, indicating the sync introduced unexpected overhead\n137\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 does not decrease by at least 50ms, indicating the pipeline stall hypothesis is wrong\n138\t- APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by more than 30ms\n139\t- Any test failure or proof verification error\n140\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BWeqBXsrkLnfa6kJtKJkxD","session_id":"00f14df6-1cef-48ea-af88-42c3b73a9be4","uuid":"f35cc9cd-4ea2-4789-b88a-88cb6f5b25ef","timestamp":"2026-04-14T22:22:25.346Z"}
